The problem is asking for the number of vans needed to transport 12 students and 3 adults, given that each van can hold 5 people. 

First, we calculate the total number of people going on the trip:
$$
12 \text{ students} + 3 \text{ adults} = 15 \text{ people}
$$

Next, we divide the total number of people by the capacity of each van:
$$
15 \text{ people} \div 5 \text{ people per van} = 3 \text{ vans}
$$

Since the division results in an exact number with no remainder, 3 vans are sufficient to transport all 15 people. There is no need to round up or consider additional constraints, as the problem does not specify any such limitations.

Thus, the number of vans needed is $\boxed{3}$.
